 da Covilhã, sometimes written Pero de Covilhã, was a Portuguese diplomat and explorer.", "newValue": "Pêro da Covilhã (c. 1450 – c. 1530) was a pivotal Portuguese diplomat, spy, and explorer whose clandestine missions in the late 15th century significantly advanced Portugal's strategic understanding of the Indian Ocean trade routes and the legendary Christian kingdom of Prester John. Operating at the vanguard of the Age of Discovery, Covilhã's reconnaissance provided critical intelligence to King John II, directly influencing Vasco da Gama's successful sea voyage to India a decade later. His unique blend of linguistic skills, resilience, and diplomatic acumen allowed him to penetrate regions previously unknown to Europeans, establishing a foundational intelligence network for Portuguese maritime expansion.

